    steps:
      - "Calculate UTC midnight difference: 30 days"
      - "Decompose into weeks: 4 weeks and 2 days"
      - "Calculate hours: 30 × 24 = 720 hours"
    result: "30 Days Remaining (4 weeks and 2 days, 720 hours, Monday)"
  - title: "New Year Countdown"
    inputs:
      - "Target Date: January 1, 2027 (Friday)"
      - "Base Date: October 24, 2026 (Saturday)"
    steps:
      - "Calculate total days: 69 calendar days"
      - "Decompose into months and days: 2 months and 8 days (9 weeks and 6 days)"
      - "Calculate hours: 69 × 24 = 1,656 hours"
    result: "69 Days Remaining (2 months and 8 days, 1,656 hours, Friday)"
  - title: "Elapsed Milestone Countdown (Past Date)"
    inputs:
      - "Target Date: September 24, 2026 (Thursday)"
      - "Base Date: October 24, 2026 (Saturday)"
    steps:
      - "Target occurs in the past: delta of -30 days"
      - "Evaluate absolute elapsed duration: 30 days ago"
      - "Decompose: 1 month ago (4 weeks and 2 days, 720 hours)"
    result: "30 Days Ago (1 month ago, 720 hours elapsed, Thursday)"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Hardcoding Static Day Counts"
    description: "Counting down to annual holidays with static estimates fails because target distances change every 24 hours. Always use dynamic calendar calculations."
  - title: "Local Timezone Date Shifting"
    description: "Comparing dates using local midnight can cause off-by-one errors across timezones. Normalizing timestamps to UTC midnight prevents shift bugs."
  - title: "Confusing Elapsed Days with Remaining Days"
    description: "Future dates yield positive counts, whereas past historical anniversaries yield elapsed days that must be labeled accordingly."
  - title: "Overlooking Month-End Variations"
    description: "Decomposing 60 days into months requires checking the actual lengths of intervening months (e.g., February has 28 or 29 days, while July and August both have 31)."
sources:
